From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Elena Maher <12779416+elenaOnline@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 04:16:53 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] gpui: Report tablet pens on mouse events on macOS

Mouse events had no way to tell a tablet's pen from a mouse, so drawing
apps could not read pen pressure. MouseDownEvent, MouseUpEvent and
MouseMoveEvent now carry `pen: Option<PenInfo>`: the tip pressure
(0..=1, 0 while hovering), the tilt in degrees and whether the eraser
end is in use, with the values and ranges of the web's PointerEvent for
`pointerType: "pen"`. Mice, trackpads and other platforms report `None`.

On macOS a mouse event is a pen's when its subtype is a tablet point or
tablet proximity, as Chromium decides (RenderWidgetHostViewCocoa
-mouseEvent:); pressure and tilt come from the tablet-point fields
(WebMouseEventBuilder::Build). Which end of the pen is in use is only in
proximity events, so the window remembers it from them (both the
NSEventTypeTabletProximity events some drivers send, now received via
-tabletProximity:, and the proximity subtype of mouse events).

A pen leaving the proximity reports MouseExited: it points nowhere,
and Chromium sends pointerleave for it.

Pen presses and releases with control held keep their button (the
left-to-right conversion for control-clicks applies to mice and
trackpads): a tablet reports the tip or eraser pressed with control, as
web engines do, and pens have their own right button.
